1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,671
Southern Italy. Seventy-one BC.

2
00:00:02,671 --> 00:00:05,272
The last morning of the slave war.

3
00:00:05,572 --> 00:00:06,993
Spartacus! What are you doing?

4
00:00:06,993 --> 00:00:07,846
That's your horse!

5
00:00:08,096 --> 00:00:09,043
If we win today,

6
00:00:09,043 --> 00:00:11,589
brother, I'll have a thousand Roman horses.

7
00:00:11,839 --> 00:00:13,055
And if we lose?

8
00:00:13,355 --> 00:00:14,810
Then I won't need one.

9
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:18,707
Across the valley, eight Roman legions are forming

10
00:00:18,707 --> 00:00:21,465
lines. They're led by the richest man in

11
00:00:21,465 --> 00:00:21,810
Rome.

12
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:23,840
Two years ago we were slaves in a

13
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,621
gladiator school. Look at us now.

14
00:00:25,871 --> 00:00:27,577
Look at them. They're afraid of us.

15
00:00:27,577 --> 00:00:28,259
Remember that.

16
00:00:28,609 --> 00:00:29,995
You are a gladiator.

17
00:00:29,995 --> 00:00:32,421
You are Spartacus's closest friend.

18
00:00:32,421 --> 00:00:35,054
And you were there from the very first

19
00:00:35,054 --> 00:00:36,926
night. To see how it began,

20
00:00:36,926 --> 00:00:38,173
go back two years.

21
00:00:40,373 --> 00:00:44,859
Capua, seventy-three BC. One of the gladiator schools

22
00:00:44,859 --> 00:00:47,822
in this city belongs to a man named

23
00:00:47,822 --> 00:00:49,346
Lentulus Batiatus.

24
00:00:49,596 --> 00:00:51,628
Teeth. Shoulders. Turn around.

25
00:00:51,628 --> 00:00:53,186
Hm. This one is strong.

26
00:00:53,186 --> 00:00:54,947
Put him with the Thracian.

27
00:00:55,197 --> 00:00:56,118
I'm not a slave!

28
00:00:56,118 --> 00:00:56,982
I was a farmer.

29
00:00:56,982 --> 00:00:58,536
Pirates took me off a ship!

30
00:00:58,786 --> 00:01:00,627
And now you belong to me.

31
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:02,100
Swear the oath, boy.

32
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,048
Every man here swore it.

33
00:01:04,048 --> 00:01:05,559
To be burned, chained,

34
00:01:05,559 --> 00:01:07,756
beaten, and killed by the sword.

35
00:01:07,756 --> 00:01:08,237
Say it!

36
00:01:08,537 --> 00:01:10,511
The gladiator's oath is real.

37
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:13,029
Roman writers quote it word for word.

38
00:01:13,279 --> 00:01:14,296
I swear to be burned,

39
00:01:14,296 --> 00:01:16,283
chained, beaten, and killed by the sword.

40
00:01:16,783 --> 00:01:19,006
That night, they lock you in a stone

41
00:01:19,006 --> 00:01:21,044
cell, smaller than a horse stall.

42
00:01:21,044 --> 00:01:22,773
Your cellmate is a Thracian.

43
00:01:23,073 --> 00:01:23,853
First night?

44
00:01:24,103 --> 00:01:25,543
Is it that obvious?

45
00:01:25,793 --> 00:01:27,507
You're shaking. I'm Spartacus.

46
00:01:27,507 --> 00:01:28,706
I was a soldier once.

47
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:30,420
Then a deserter. Then a slave.

48
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:32,224
How long have you been here?

49
00:01:32,474 --> 00:01:34,217
Long enough to count the guards.

50
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:35,579
Get some sleep. Tomorrow,

51
00:01:35,579 --> 00:01:37,431
the doctore will try to break you.

52
00:01:37,731 --> 00:01:39,839
Thracian! Teaching the new puppy to bark?

53
00:01:40,089 --> 00:01:42,103
Crixus. Go back to your corner.

54
00:01:42,353 --> 00:01:44,915
Ha! Welcome to the house of Batiatus,

55
00:01:44,915 --> 00:01:47,616
puppy. Most of you don't last a season.

56
00:01:49,816 --> 00:01:51,538
Your days begin at dawn.

57
00:01:51,538 --> 00:01:52,757
Every single day.

58
00:01:53,007 --> 00:01:55,006
Wooden swords! To the post!

59
00:01:55,006 --> 00:01:56,560
Strike! Again! Again!

60
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,986
Twice the weight of a real sword!

61
00:01:58,986 --> 00:02:01,305
In the arena, the real one will feel

62
00:02:01,305 --> 00:02:02,271
like a feather!

63
00:02:02,521 --> 00:02:04,809
The post was called the palus.

64
00:02:04,809 --> 00:02:08,470
Roman writers say recruits used weapons twice as

65
00:02:08,470 --> 00:02:09,919
heavy as real ones.

66
00:02:10,169 --> 00:02:12,205
My arms. I can't lift them anymore.

67
00:02:12,455 --> 00:02:14,677
The crowd pays to see you fight,

68
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:16,066
not to see you rest!

69
00:02:16,066 --> 00:02:17,247
Shield up! Again!

70
00:02:17,747 --> 00:02:18,796
Here. Eat. You'll need it.

71
00:02:19,046 --> 00:02:20,059
What is this?

72
00:02:20,309 --> 00:02:21,799
Barley and beans. Every day.

73
00:02:21,799 --> 00:02:23,290
They call us the barley men.

74
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:26,302
That nickname is real: hordearii.

75
00:02:26,302 --> 00:02:30,247
Bones from a gladiator graveyard in Ephesus show

76
00:02:30,247 --> 00:02:32,959
they ate mostly grains and beans.

77
00:02:33,209 --> 00:02:34,893
And drink this after training.

78
00:02:34,893 --> 00:02:35,960
Plant ash in water.

79
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:37,242
Ugh! It's bitter!

80
00:02:37,492 --> 00:02:39,364
Bitter, but it makes your bones strong.

81
00:02:39,364 --> 00:02:40,516
The doctors swear by it.

82
00:02:40,816 --> 00:02:41,767
They want us fat,

83
00:02:41,767 --> 00:02:42,998
too. Fat protects you.

84
00:02:42,998 --> 00:02:45,013
A cut bleeds, but you keep fighting.

85
00:02:45,263 --> 00:02:47,345
So they feed us like prize horses.

86
00:02:47,595 --> 00:02:48,790
We are prize horses.

87
00:02:48,790 --> 00:02:50,403
Batiatus paid a lot for us.

88
00:02:50,403 --> 00:02:51,836
He doesn't want us dead.

89
00:02:51,836 --> 00:02:53,868
He wants us bleeding in front of a

90
00:02:53,868 --> 00:02:54,226
crowd.

91
00:02:54,726 --> 00:02:56,424
You! New one! Curved sword,

92
00:02:56,424 --> 00:02:59,506
small shield. You'll fight in the Thracian style.

93
00:02:59,756 --> 00:03:01,018
Like Spartacus?

94
00:03:01,268 --> 00:03:03,603
Like my people. The Romans named a whole

95
00:03:03,603 --> 00:03:05,062
way of fighting after us.

96
00:03:05,362 --> 00:03:08,132
Back then, the three main styles were named

97
00:03:08,132 --> 00:03:10,902
after peoples Rome had beaten: the Samnite,

98
00:03:10,902 --> 00:03:12,641
the Gaul, and the Thracian.

99
00:03:12,941 --> 00:03:13,958
And I'm the Gaul.

100
00:03:13,958 --> 00:03:15,574
Big shield, straight sword.

101
00:03:15,574 --> 00:03:17,130
When we meet in the arena,

102
00:03:17,130 --> 00:03:18,447
puppy, try not to cry.

103
00:03:20,647 --> 00:03:22,940
Three months later. Your first games.

104
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:25,907
People of Capua! The house of Batiatus presents

105
00:03:25,907 --> 00:03:27,294
ten pairs of gladiators!

106
00:03:27,594 --> 00:03:30,159
First the parade. Animal hunts in the morning.

107
00:03:30,159 --> 00:03:32,669
Criminals at noon. We fight in the afternoon.

108
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:34,343
There are thousands of them.

109
00:03:34,343 --> 00:03:36,276
Women. Children. Old men eating bread.

110
00:03:36,526 --> 00:03:37,741
They're not here for you.

111
00:03:37,741 --> 00:03:39,199
They're here for a good fight.

112
00:03:39,199 --> 00:03:40,560
Give them one, and you live.

113
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:43,233
First, a warm-up with blunt weapons.

114
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:45,210
Then the real swords come out.

115
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,844
Gladiators! Fight clean. When I raise my stick,

116
00:03:47,844 --> 00:03:48,656
you stop. Begin!

117
00:03:48,956 --> 00:03:50,652
Yes, there was a referee.

118
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:53,434
Roman mosaics show him with a long stick,

119
00:03:53,434 --> 00:03:55,266
right between the fighters.

120
00:03:55,516 --> 00:03:57,918
Come on, Thracian! Show me that little sword!

121
00:03:58,168 --> 00:03:59,551
Shield up. Watch his feet.

122
00:03:59,551 --> 00:04:00,349
Watch his feet.

123
00:04:00,649 --> 00:04:02,396
His shield covers everything!

124
00:04:02,396 --> 00:04:03,842
Go low! Go for the legs!

125
00:04:04,142 --> 00:04:05,058
Agh! My leg!

126
00:04:05,308 --> 00:04:06,883
He is down! He is down!

127
00:04:07,183 --> 00:04:08,592
Stop! He raises a finger!

128
00:04:08,592 --> 00:04:09,606
He asks for mercy!

129
00:04:09,606 --> 00:04:10,677
The editor decides!

130
00:04:10,927 --> 00:04:13,208
The editor was the rich man who paid

131
00:04:13,208 --> 00:04:15,615
for the games. The loser's life was in

132
00:04:15,615 --> 00:04:16,249
his hands.

133
00:04:16,499 --> 00:04:17,899
Do I have to kill him?

134
00:04:18,149 --> 00:04:19,180
Look at the crowd.

135
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:20,555
Watch the editor's hand.

136
00:04:20,855 --> 00:04:23,838
Movies show a thumb pointing down for death.

137
00:04:23,838 --> 00:04:26,482
That idea comes from a painting made in

138
00:04:26,482 --> 00:04:30,347
eighteen seventy-two. Nobody knows exactly what the Roman

139
00:04:30,347 --> 00:04:31,703
gesture looked like.

140
00:04:32,003 --> 00:04:33,530
The editor grants mercy!

141
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:33,976
Missio!

142
00:04:34,226 --> 00:04:35,558
He lives. I live.

143
00:04:35,908 --> 00:04:38,336
Most fights did not end in death.

144
00:04:38,336 --> 00:04:42,384
Gladiators were expensive. Records from a century later

145
00:04:42,384 --> 00:04:45,475
suggest about one fight in five ended with

146
00:04:45,475 --> 00:04:46,284
a dead man.

147
00:04:46,534 --> 00:04:47,761
One in five, brother.

148
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:49,688
Fight ten times, and do the math.

149
00:04:49,938 --> 00:04:50,976
That's not a life.

150
00:04:50,976 --> 00:04:52,360
That's a slow execution.

151
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:54,534
Exactly. So let's not stay.

152
00:04:56,734 --> 00:05:00,921
Summer, seventy-three BC. Two hundred gladiators are planning

153
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:01,607
an escape.

154
00:05:01,857 --> 00:05:03,465
Tonight. The kitchen. Knives,

155
00:05:03,465 --> 00:05:06,127
cleavers, roasting spits. Anything with an edge.

156
00:05:06,377 --> 00:05:08,540
Kitchen knives against Roman swords?

157
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:09,802
You're mad, Thracian.

158
00:05:10,052 --> 00:05:11,405
You have a better plan,

159
00:05:11,405 --> 00:05:13,228
Gaul? Or do you want to die for

160
00:05:13,228 --> 00:05:14,228
Batiatus's money?

161
00:05:14,528 --> 00:05:15,599
Hm. I'm in.

162
00:05:15,949 --> 00:05:18,418
But someone talks. The guards find out.

163
00:05:18,668 --> 00:05:20,444
They know! Spartacus, they know!

164
00:05:20,644 --> 00:05:22,258
Then it's now or never!

165
00:05:22,258 --> 00:05:22,749
Go! Go!

166
00:05:23,099 --> 00:05:25,740
Of two hundred, only about seventy make it

167
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:27,627
out, armed with kitchen tools.

168
00:05:27,927 --> 00:05:28,763
Look! On the road!

169
00:05:28,763 --> 00:05:29,134
A wagon!

170
00:05:29,384 --> 00:05:31,267
It's full of gladiator weapons!

171
00:05:31,267 --> 00:05:31,996
Real swords!

172
00:05:32,246 --> 00:05:33,975
Throw away the kitchen knives,

173
00:05:33,975 --> 00:05:35,877
brothers. We're gladiators again.

174
00:05:35,877 --> 00:05:38,067
But this time, we fight for ourselves.

175
00:05:40,267 --> 00:05:42,943
They hide on a mountain near Naples.

176
00:05:42,943 --> 00:05:45,172
You know it today as Vesuvius.

177
00:05:45,172 --> 00:05:48,071
In seventy-three BC, it's just a quiet,

178
00:05:48,071 --> 00:05:49,186
green mountain.

179
00:05:49,436 --> 00:05:51,135
Three thousand men. One road up.

180
00:05:51,135 --> 00:05:53,419
We don't attack. We wait until they starve.

181
00:05:53,719 --> 00:05:57,024
The praetor Claudius Glaber blocks the only path.

182
00:05:57,024 --> 00:05:59,789
Behind the rebels, there are only cliffs.

183
00:06:00,289 --> 00:06:01,939
Spartacus. I dreamed of you.

184
00:06:01,939 --> 00:06:04,354
A snake was coiled around your face while

185
00:06:04,354 --> 00:06:04,943
you slept.

186
00:06:05,193 --> 00:06:06,633
Is that good, or bad?

187
00:06:06,883 --> 00:06:08,947
Both. Great power. And a terrible end.

188
00:06:09,247 --> 00:06:11,671
Plutarch really tells this story.

189
00:06:11,671 --> 00:06:14,829
Spartacus's wife was a Thracian prophetess.

190
00:06:15,329 --> 00:06:17,655
These wild vines. Look how thick they are.

191
00:06:17,655 --> 00:06:19,482
Spartacus, we could make ladders!

192
00:06:19,732 --> 00:06:21,145
Ladders. Down the cliff.

193
00:06:21,145 --> 00:06:22,617
Brother, you're a genius.

194
00:06:22,917 --> 00:06:25,986
That night they weave ladders from wild grapevines,

195
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:28,272
and climb down the cliffs in the dark.

196
00:06:28,572 --> 00:06:30,471
What's that noise? Who's out there?

197
00:06:30,671 --> 00:06:31,669
Now! Attack!

198
00:06:31,969 --> 00:06:33,565
They're running! Take their swords!

199
00:06:33,565 --> 00:06:34,340
Take their armor!

200
00:06:34,590 --> 00:06:36,987
Attacked from behind, the Romans flee.

201
00:06:36,987 --> 00:06:38,880
The slaves take their weapons,

202
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:40,205
and their whole camp.

203
00:06:42,405 --> 00:06:45,562
Word spreads. Slaves run away from farms and

204
00:06:45,562 --> 00:06:47,786
villas all over southern Italy.

205
00:06:48,036 --> 00:06:50,583
Spartacus, look! There are hundreds more on the

206
00:06:50,583 --> 00:06:51,720
road. Women. Old men.

207
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:52,208
Children.

208
00:06:52,458 --> 00:06:54,422
Let them come. Every one of them was

209
00:06:54,422 --> 00:06:55,350
chained, like us.

210
00:06:55,650 --> 00:06:56,833
We need iron. Swords,

211
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:57,621
armor, horses.

212
00:06:57,871 --> 00:06:59,073
Then we take iron.

213
00:06:59,073 --> 00:07:00,609
But no gold. No silver.

214
00:07:00,609 --> 00:07:01,944
Gold makes men lazy.

215
00:07:02,244 --> 00:07:06,015
Appian writes that Spartacus banned merchants from bringing

216
00:07:06,015 --> 00:07:07,869
gold or silver into the camp,

217
00:07:07,869 --> 00:07:09,659
and shared the loot equally.

218
00:07:09,959 --> 00:07:12,646
Not everyone lived to see what came next.

219
00:07:12,646 --> 00:07:15,202
Oenomaus, who gave you his bowl on your

220
00:07:15,202 --> 00:07:17,824
first day, was killed in the first year.

221
00:07:18,224 --> 00:07:20,191
But the army keeps growing.

222
00:07:20,191 --> 00:07:23,324
Seventy thousand people, led by gladiators.

223
00:07:23,324 --> 00:07:26,165
They beat one Roman army after another.

224
00:07:26,415 --> 00:07:27,385
We beat them again!

225
00:07:27,385 --> 00:07:28,712
Two praetors! Two consuls!

226
00:07:28,962 --> 00:07:30,253
And every time we win,

227
00:07:30,253 --> 00:07:31,662
they send a bigger army.

228
00:07:33,862 --> 00:07:37,536
Seventy-two BC. The leaders argue about what to

229
00:07:37,536 --> 00:07:38,161
do next.

230
00:07:38,411 --> 00:07:40,013
We go north. Over the Alps.

231
00:07:40,013 --> 00:07:41,437
Then everyone goes home.

232
00:07:41,437 --> 00:07:42,565
Thrace. Gaul. Free.

233
00:07:42,815 --> 00:07:44,431
Home? Why run? Rome is weak!

234
00:07:44,431 --> 00:07:46,682
We should burn every villa from here to

235
00:07:46,682 --> 00:07:47,144
the sea!

236
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:49,641
And then what? Rome never runs out of

237
00:07:49,641 --> 00:07:51,098
soldiers, Crixus. We do.

238
00:07:51,348 --> 00:07:53,096
Then I'll take my Gauls and do it

239
00:07:53,096 --> 00:07:53,467
myself.

240
00:07:53,717 --> 00:07:56,292
Crixus, don't. Together we're seventy thousand.

241
00:07:56,292 --> 00:07:57,936
Apart, we're two easy targets.

242
00:07:58,186 --> 00:07:59,436
You're his dog, puppy.

243
00:07:59,436 --> 00:08:01,141
You always were. Come with me,

244
00:08:01,141 --> 00:08:01,937
and be a wolf.

245
00:08:02,337 --> 00:08:03,733
So, who do you follow?

246
00:08:03,733 --> 00:08:06,144
Your best friend, or the man who wants

247
00:08:06,144 --> 00:08:06,652
revenge?

248
00:08:07,052 --> 00:08:08,585
I stay with Spartacus.

249
00:08:08,835 --> 00:08:10,295
Then good luck. Both of you.

250
00:08:10,795 --> 00:08:14,102
Crixus left with about thirty thousand men.

251
00:08:14,102 --> 00:08:17,333
Near Mount Garganus, a consul caught them.

252
00:08:17,333 --> 00:08:20,179
Crixus and two thirds of his men were

253
00:08:20,179 --> 00:08:20,717
killed.

254
00:08:21,067 --> 00:08:22,869
He's dead. Crixus is dead.

255
00:08:23,169 --> 00:08:24,691
Then Rome will pay for him.

256
00:08:24,691 --> 00:08:26,101
The way they made us pay.

257
00:08:26,401 --> 00:08:29,871
Appian writes that Spartacus held funeral games for

258
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:33,410
Crixus. Three hundred Roman prisoners were forced to

259
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:35,723
fight each other, like gladiators.

260
00:08:36,023 --> 00:08:37,776
Now they know what it feels like.

261
00:08:38,076 --> 00:08:39,049
Now they know.

262
00:08:41,249 --> 00:08:44,275
Rome is terrified. The richest man in the

263
00:08:44,275 --> 00:08:46,490
city, Marcus Licinius Crassus,

264
00:08:46,490 --> 00:08:47,523
takes command.

265
00:08:47,773 --> 00:08:50,449
Some of my men ran away from slaves.

266
00:08:50,449 --> 00:08:53,050
Line them up. Every tenth man dies.

267
00:08:53,350 --> 00:08:55,226
This was called decimation.

268
00:08:55,226 --> 00:08:58,632
Plutarch says fifty soldiers were killed by their

269
00:08:58,632 --> 00:08:59,535
own comrades.

270
00:08:59,785 --> 00:09:01,987
His soldiers are more afraid of him than

271
00:09:01,987 --> 00:09:02,538
of us now.

272
00:09:03,038 --> 00:09:04,897
Spartacus heads for the sea.

273
00:09:04,897 --> 00:09:06,755
He wants to cross to Sicily,

274
00:09:06,755 --> 00:09:09,477
where thousands of slaves could join him.

275
00:09:09,727 --> 00:09:12,170
Pay us, and our ships will carry your

276
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:14,151
army across. You have my word.

277
00:09:14,401 --> 00:09:15,638
Then we have a deal.

278
00:09:15,988 --> 00:09:17,635
The pirates take the money,

279
00:09:17,635 --> 00:09:19,830
and sail away. They never come back.

280
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:21,802
They're gone! The ships are gone!

281
00:09:22,202 --> 00:09:24,308
Dig. A ditch from sea to sea,

282
00:09:24,308 --> 00:09:25,833
and a wall behind it.

283
00:09:25,833 --> 00:09:26,995
Let them starve.

284
00:09:27,245 --> 00:09:29,879
Crassus cut off the whole toe of Italy.

285
00:09:29,879 --> 00:09:33,594
Plutarch says the ditch was about fifty-five kilometers

286
00:09:33,594 --> 00:09:33,932
long.

287
00:09:34,182 --> 00:09:35,965
He's trapped us. Sea on three sides,

288
00:09:35,965 --> 00:09:37,005
a wall on the fourth.

289
00:09:37,255 --> 00:09:38,971
Then we wait for the right night.

290
00:09:39,271 --> 00:09:40,685
On a snowy winter night,

291
00:09:40,685 --> 00:09:42,923
the rebels fill part of the ditch with

292
00:09:42,923 --> 00:09:45,162
earth and branches, and break through.

293
00:09:45,412 --> 00:09:46,977
We're out! We're through the wall!

294
00:09:49,177 --> 00:09:52,347
Spring, seventy-one BC. And now you're back on

295
00:09:52,347 --> 00:09:53,588
that last morning.

296
00:09:53,888 --> 00:09:55,359
Spartacus. If this is the end,

297
00:09:55,359 --> 00:09:57,321
thank you. For the night in the kitchen.

298
00:09:57,321 --> 00:09:58,008
For all of it.

299
00:09:58,258 --> 00:09:59,422
Don't thank me, brother.

300
00:09:59,422 --> 00:10:00,974
You were free before you met me.

301
00:10:00,974 --> 00:10:02,381
They just forgot to tell you.

302
00:10:02,681 --> 00:10:04,188
Today, I want Crassus.

303
00:10:04,188 --> 00:10:05,078
Only Crassus.

304
00:10:05,478 --> 00:10:08,896
Plutarch writes that Spartacus fought his way toward

305
00:10:08,896 --> 00:10:11,656
Crassus himself. He killed two centurions.

306
00:10:11,656 --> 00:10:13,036
He never reached him.

307
00:10:13,286 --> 00:10:14,765
Spartacus! Where are you?

308
00:10:14,765 --> 00:10:15,357
Spartacus!

309
00:10:15,707 --> 00:10:17,784
He was surrounded and cut down.

310
00:10:17,784 --> 00:10:19,459
His body was never found.

311
00:10:20,059 --> 00:10:23,893
Six thousand captured rebels were crucified along the

312
00:10:23,893 --> 00:10:26,786
Appian Way, the road from Capua to Rome.

313
00:10:26,786 --> 00:10:29,173
For about two hundred kilometers,

314
00:10:29,173 --> 00:10:31,126
the crosses lined the road.

315
00:10:31,526 --> 00:10:32,868
I run into the mountains.

316
00:10:32,868 --> 00:10:33,941
A few of us made it.

317
00:10:33,941 --> 00:10:34,907
Most of us didn't.

318
00:10:35,207 --> 00:10:38,378
Pompey caught five thousand more running north,

319
00:10:38,378 --> 00:10:40,739
and told Rome he had ended the war.

320
00:10:40,739 --> 00:10:42,493
Crassus never forgave him.

321
00:10:44,693 --> 00:10:46,342
So, how much of this is true?

322
00:10:46,592 --> 00:10:49,542
Almost everything we know comes from two Greek

323
00:10:49,542 --> 00:10:51,402
writers, Plutarch and Appian.

324
00:10:51,402 --> 00:10:53,711
They wrote more than a century and a

325
00:10:53,711 --> 00:10:54,416
half later.

326
00:10:54,666 --> 00:10:56,843
So nobody who was there wrote it down?

327
00:10:57,093 --> 00:10:59,892
Nothing that survived. But in Pompeii,

328
00:10:59,892 --> 00:11:03,355
archaeologists found a painted fight on a wall.

329
00:11:03,355 --> 00:11:06,891
Above one rider, someone wrote a name: Spartaks.

330
00:11:07,141 --> 00:11:09,906
Spartaks. Painted while people still remembered him?

331
00:11:10,156 --> 00:11:12,135
Maybe. It's written in Oscan,

332
00:11:12,135 --> 00:11:14,182
an old language of the region,

333
00:11:14,182 --> 00:11:16,638
and the painting may be from his own

334
00:11:16,638 --> 00:11:17,252
lifetime.

335
00:11:17,552 --> 00:11:20,459
And the famous scene where every slave stands

336
00:11:20,459 --> 00:11:22,333
up and shouts, I'm Spartacus?

337
00:11:22,333 --> 00:11:25,111
That's from a movie made in nineteen sixty.

338
00:11:25,111 --> 00:11:26,274
It never happened.

339
00:11:26,574 --> 00:11:27,838
Two years. A stone cell,

340
00:11:27,838 --> 00:11:29,786
a kitchen knife, and a ladder made of

341
00:11:29,786 --> 00:11:31,997
vines. And the most powerful city on Earth

342
00:11:31,997 --> 00:11:32,892
was afraid of us.

343
00:11:33,242 --> 00:11:36,106
So, if you'd been there in seventy-two BC,

344
00:11:36,106 --> 00:11:38,833
would you have followed Spartacus north,

345
00:11:38,833 --> 00:11:40,538
or Crixus into the fight?

346
00:11:40,538 --> 00:11:42,174
Tell me in the comments.
